From: Ben Carter <ben_carter@redwood.com>
To: Sam Li <sam.li@sequoia-thera.com>, Priya Natarajan <priya_natarajan@redwood.com>
Cc: Grace O'Connor <grace_oconnor@redwood.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Sep 2026 08:42:00 -07:00
Subject: Re: Integration hypothesis & prioritized risks — Redwood SDK / embeddings / structured outputs

Hey Sam / team —

Following up with a concrete tech approach and a small example so your engineers can evaluate feasibility quickly. High-level suggestions below; happy to run a 30-minute tech deep-dive next week.

Technical approach
- Structured output: we recommend function-calling semantics where the model emits a validated JSON blob. For determinism, Redwood runtime will validate against a provided JSON schema and reject or tag results that fail validation.
- Streaming: implement chunked JSON tokens (we stream partial objects along "output fragments"). The SDK exposes a streaming parser that reconstructs and validates objects client-side as chunks arrive to keep perceived latency low.
- Embeddings + rerank: produce 1536-d embeddings using the rdx-embed-1536 model, index in FAISS; reranker runs a small cross-encoder (rdx-rerank-small) over top-20 candidates to improve clinical relevance.
- Cache/batching: prefix/KV caching for repeated prompts (e.g., same patient summary context) and micro-batching on the service side to hit cost/latency targets. For the POC, we can tune batch window to ~10ms to reduce cost while keeping median latency target.
- Quantization: recommend int8 paths for dedicated capacity to reduce memory while keeping quality; we'll run a quick eval on a subset of your prompt corpus.
- Networking & security: we require VPC peering (or VPC connector) + customer KMS wrapped keys for any PHI processing. Audit logs exposed to your S3 bucket or Syslog endpoint; retention configurable.

Tiny SDK snippet (conceptual, TypeScript-like)

const stream = redwood.sdk.stream({
  model: 'rdx-structured-1',
  stream: true,
  function_schema: 'https://drive.google.com/your-schema.json',
});

stream.on('data', (fragment) => {
  // SDK incremental parser validates fragments against schema
  parser.push(fragment);
  const obj = parser.maybeGetComplete();
  if (obj) {
    // deterministic JSON object ready for EHR ingest
    sendToEHR(obj);
  }
});

POC success criteria we propose
- 90%+ schema validation pass rate across 500 sample summaries
- Median end-to-end latency <= 200ms for single-turn summary path
- Top-5 rerank relevance improvement > 18% vs baseline (internal metric)

I'll attach the topology diagram (redwood_integration_sketch.png) and a sample JSON schema in the drive link earlier. If these approaches look good we can slot a POC kick-off on Oct 6 — I'll hold time for an architecture call.

